From 23bde56643c9cc88b247ef62f1cbc217d6654f2c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 09:52:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] sarp: linter compatibility for the compact options

Without this, `ruff format` and `ruff check --fix` fight each other: the
formatter emits compact output and pycodestyle, flake8-commas and isort
immediately flag or undo it.

Each option relaxes exactly the rules it contradicts, wired up in
`ruff_workspace`'s fork-owned `compact` module:

    annotation-spacing    E225 (->), E231 (annotation colons, including
                          variable and class-attribute annotations)
    operator-spacing      E225/E226/E227/E228, including division's own
                          PEP 570 branch
    dict-spacing          E231 in dict literals
    default-value-spacing E251
    import-trailing-comma COM812, and isort's own emitter
    top-level-blank-lines E302/E303/E305 and isort's lines-after-imports
    nested-blank-lines    E301/E306

The isort part matters because I001 rewrites wrapped import blocks itself and
appended a trailing comma unconditionally, so it kept restoring the comma the
formatter had just removed. `emit_trailing_comma` (default true) suppresses it.

E231's relaxation is deliberately narrow: it applies to annotation colons only.
Compound statements (`if x:pass`), the soft keywords `match`/`case`/`type`, and
lambdas all still report.
